## Hypothesis ID and title

**H-002 — DOM hierarchy and content extraction**

## Research question

How does DOM hierarchy affect document segmentation and extraction of content relationships?

## Hypothesis statement

For equivalent text, a DOM with clear, consistent section and heading containment may support more accurate association of content with its section than a hierarchy with ambiguous or excessively nested boundaries.

## Theoretical background

DOM parent-child relationships and heading placement are inspectable structure. Their downstream use varies by parser and must be measured rather than assumed.

## Supporting literature and existing evidence

Not yet assessed; no evidence or source records are linked.

## Expected outcome

In the tested parsers, explicitly bounded sections may yield fewer section-assignment errors than the selected comparison structures.

## Alternative explanations and potential counterarguments

Text order, heading wording, parser heuristics, chunking policy, or model behavior may dominate the effect. “Clear” hierarchy requires an operational definition.

## Validation approach

Operationalize hierarchy variants and annotation criteria in [EXP-001](/en/05-experiments/exp-001/) and [EXP-004](/en/05-experiments/exp-004/). Keep text constant and compare structure-specific outcomes.

## Related experiments and findings

- Proposed: EXP-001 and EXP-004.
- Findings: none recorded.
